  "meta": {
    "name": "gpt-oss-20b",
    "family": "gpt-oss",
    "notes": "OpenAI's smaller open-weight model: 21B parameters, 32 experts a layer and four of them active. Two things set its attention apart, and both are written into the design. Every other layer attends only within a 128-token band, starting with the first, so the repeating unit is the pair and the stack repeats L/2 times, as Gemma 2's does. And every attention layer has sinks: one learned score per head that sits in the softmax's denominator beside the keys, so a head can put its attention nowhere rather than spreading it over keys it has no use for — 64 parameters a layer, which the published count includes. Biases on every projection, the router included, and on every expert. OpenAI's 3.61B active is this design's active count without the embedding table — the unembedding is a matmul every token pays for, the lookup is not. Two things are recorded but not generated: YaRN's rotary scaling, which stretches a 4k-trained model to 128k, and the experts' activation, which is SwiGLU with a sigmoid slope of 1.702, the linear half offset by one and both halves clamped at 7. Both change what the model computes and not what it costs; the generated model uses plain RoPE and SiLU.",
    "published": {
      "params": 20914757184,
      "source": "https://huggingface.co/openai/gpt-oss-20b"
    }
  },
